STATEN ISLAND, N.Y. - Fifty years of being saddled with the world's largest landfill didn't defile Staten Island enough, it seems.
Now it turns out the state Department of Environmental Conservation has turned one of our pristine outdoor areas into a dumping ground for deer carcasses.
You cannot make this stuff up.
It all started when Staten Island wildlife photographer Tom Puma found bones and decaying deer carcasses at the Mount Loretto Unique Area, a state park in Pleasant Plains. He saw three carcasses in addition to a lot of bones. Some of the deer had all or some of their heads missing.
"It looked like a fallout, a mass suicide or something," he said, "it was really bizarre."
Another Staten Islander who came across the bones and teeth and skulls during a stroll through the area wondered if she'd stumbled onto a site used for devil worship.
Hey, stranger things have happened on Staten Island. And this is the wilderness part of the borough.
Here's where it gets good: Puma was told by a forest ranger that the area was a disposal site for deer hit by cars on Staten Island.
By the time we got to the area to have a look for ourselves, the fresher carcasses that Puma had documented were gone. There was a patch of dirt that looked like it had been freshly overturned. And some truck tire tracks. So maybe the dead deer had been buried, as the DEC said was their plan. Or maybe they'd been carted off.
But there were plenty of bones left scattered around. Jawbones with the teeth intact. A sun-bleached skull that looked straight out of a Georgia O'Keefe painting. A nearly intact spinal column. It wasn't hard to find them. You had to be careful where you stepped. Definitely a "Pet Sematary" vibe.
The Advance was told that the deer were improperly disposed of by DEC staff. Deceased deer are supposed to be buried, incinerated or disposed of in landfills, according to DEC recommendations.
But in this instance, DEC said the deer remains were placed in a "remote area" of the Mount Loretto park. Remote area? Not quite. The makeshift deer graveyard was just a few feet from a main path in the park, a short walk away from the much visited stone shrine dedicated to the Virgin Mary.
Is this the purpose of our parklands and open spaces? To serve as a convenient dumping ground for dead animal carcasses? Imagine if you were walking in the park and came upon this carnage. Imagine little kids on a school field trip.
