o, from the comfort of your home, by live-stream, in real time (http://www.meetview.com/neb/index.php?enter). It is remarkable to hear speaker after speaker, many highly expert in areas of construction, seismology, coastal ecology, navigation, finance, insurance, etc., all sharply critical of the Northern Gateway proposal. The critiques are articulate and thoughtful. Many ��non-expert�� passionate members of the public also are presenting. I have yet to hear a presenter in favour of the project. Where is the mainstream media coverage? Is The Vancouver Sun ignoring the content of this panel review intentionally? These proceedings and the overwhelming response of the participants should be on the front page every day. CHARLES KING Surrey Skills assessment is needed to monitor students�� progress Re: FSA helps ensure resources are directed where they��re needed, Don McRae, Letters, Jan. 14 I was very pleased to learn that our current minister of education, Don McRae, has come out in support of the annual Foundation Skills Assessment (FSA). Although the BCTF continues to pressure governments to eliminate the FSAs and go back to the outdated and useless random sampling system, I know from personal experience as a former inner city principal that the FSAs help to keep everyone accountable. They provide important information on how all students are doing in basic skills at Grade 4 and Grade 7 and they are the only source of public information on how schools and school districts are doing in terms of educating aboriginal students.
