Q. I have been trying to find out who sang I Hear a Waltz and in what movie. My friend says she remembers it well and her husband kids her and says she made it up. Who is right? I'd like to prove her right as I also seem to remember that song being sung in a movie, but can't place it. It may have been I Hum a Waltz. -- Millie Sobel, Margate
A. The correct title is I Hum a Waltz. It was written in 1937 by Harry Revel and Mack Gordon. It was introduced by Barbara Stanwyck in the movie This Is My Affair.
Q. I remember a song from the '40s called Have You Changed. I'm guessing Freddie Martin's Orchestra recorded it. Was Buddy Clark the vocalist and can it be found on cassette and where? -- Pete Zlonkevicz, Wilmington, Del.
A. Freddie Martin did indeed record Have You Changed, on Bluebird No. 11311, but Buddy Clark was not the vocalist. Gene Krupa and his orchestra also recorded it for Okeh Records, No. 3606, with an excellent vocal by Howard DuLany. Both of these are available at Records Revisited, 34 W. 33rd St., New York 10001; 1-212-695-7155.
Q. We have been looking for a record titled Going Home. We want a vocal, not an instrumental. Do you know if anyone recorded this? -- Mr. and Mrs. Joe Perconti, Pompano Beach
A. This 1922 song written by William Arms Fisher was recorded in May 1927 by singer Richard Crooks with The EverReady Group on a 12-inch 78 rpm on Victor No. 35823. It is being held for you at Records Revisited mentioned in the previous answer.
Q. About a song called Doodle-E-Do, I found out from a friend it was popular in the '20s and '30s. I was able to get the words and thought you might recognize the song if you saw the lyrics: "Please play for me that sweet melody called doodle-de-do, doodle-de-do; I like the rest but what I like best is doodle-de-do, doodle-de-do," etc. Hope in your travels you may come across a tape that I could purchase. -- Fred Hess, Hillsboro Beach
A. What stumped me on this was the fact that the correct title is Doodle Doo Doo, despite that the lyrics are sung as "doodle-de-do." In fact, Morty Savada at Records Revisited played the record of it over the phone and the lyrics are indeed exactly as you indicated. The record I heard was by Tiny Hill and his Orchestra, recorded in 1939 on Vocalion No. 5060. This song was written in 1924 by Mel Stitzel and band leader Art Kassel, who used it as his theme song. Several others recorded it, including Eddie Cantor, and I suggest that you contact Records Revisited about your preference. Their address and phone number are shown elsewhere in this column.
